{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Woe to you, O Moab.</b> He pronounced a curse upon him, that he be delivered into his hand. <b>Chemosh.</b> The name of the god of Moab. <b>Gave his sons.</b> The one who gave — he gave his sons. <b>As fugitives (פלטים).</b> Survivors of the sword, that is, fleeing. Or: 'gave' refers back to Chemosh — Scripture says that the people of Moab were worshippers of Chemosh their god, building high places and trusting in him more than any people, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 16:12) ״והיה כי נראה כי נלאה מואב אל הבמה״ and so forth, and it is written (Jeremiah 48:7) ״ויצא כמוש בגולה״; and therefore the rulers would say by way of mockery that Chemosh gave his sons as fugitives and his daughters into captivity, just as they are called by his name, and thus it is written (Malachi 2:11) ״ובעל בת אל נכר״; so explained the Ramban, of blessed memory."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:46.285970197Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}